I've committed the change (3ac62098). From now on, /cost bills Google's introductory rate on the four Gemini rows until each promo ends, then switches to the regular rate. Build, all 7003 tests, clippy with -D warnings and the fmt check pass. The #1012 comment did not post: gh isn't logged in here, so the before/after counts and the "Next" line are only in the commit message for now.

The rates. I re-checked Google's pricing page today (2026-10-28):

| Model | Introductory (input / cache read / output) | Until | Regular rate after |
|---|---|---|---|
| gemini-4-argon | $2 / $0.10 / $10 | 2026-11-30 | $4 / $0.20 / $20 |
| gemini-3.8-flash, gemini-3.6-flash, and gemini-3.7-flash (Google sends it to 3.8) | $0.75 / $0.075 / $3.75 | 2026-12-31 | $1.50 / $0.15 / $7.50 |

How it works
• Lookup: the new file src/format/cost/promo.rs holds a pure function promo_pricing_at(model, now). It matches exact ids only, so gemini-3.8-flash-tts doesn't get the promo rate. The price lookup reads the clock and checks this first. A user's own [model_pricing] price still wins, and a google/ prefix bills the same as the bare id.
• End times: Google gives dates but no time zone, so each promo ends at midnight UTC on the first regular day, the earliest reading. If Google means a later zone, the bill reads high for those hours, never low. A clock set before 1970 also gets the regular (higher) rate.
• One known side effect: costs are priced when they're shown. A November session viewed in December shows the regular rate, which again reads high.
• Line cap: src/format/cost.rs is still at exactly its 3640-line limit. I made room by shortening comments.

Tests
• Each row gets the promo rate one second before its end date and the regular rate at the end, checked through the same price lookup /cost uses.
• Argon's promo ends a month before the Flash rows.
• Similar-looking ids never get a promo rate, and their price doesn't change with the date.
• Three existing tests that check the regular rate now use a fixed date after all promos end. Otherwise they would have broken today, since they read the real clock.
• I disabled the promo match on purpose: 3 of the 6 new tests failed by name, and all passed again after I restored it.

Price audit against models.dev (network test, run by hand):
```
before: compared 114, matched 75, drifted 26
after:  compared 114, matched 79, drifted 22   (google 4 → 0)
```
Remaining mismatches: openai 16, zai 5, deepseek 1. I checked each one against src/providers.rs: the only one users can pick from yoyo's model lists is deepseek-v4-pro, which already has its reason recorded. So there are 0 pickable defects left, and the title stays "Part of #1012".

Docs: in cost-tracking.md, the error-direction rule now says to model a price variation when what decides it (date, endpoint, prompt size) reaches yoyo, and to use the higher rate only when it doesn't. It also has a table of the date-based rows. models.md no longer says these two Gemini models bill the regular rate during the promo.

Not covered: Google applies the same promo window to TTS and robotics models, which yoyo doesn't price exactly. It also applies to the Batch, Flex and Priority tiers, which yoyo never uses.

